{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And the sense of \"father of all the children of Ever.\"</b> To make known the father of the Hebrews, for there is none above him, and HaShem is his God. And so it is written: \"Thus says YHWH, God of the Hebrews\" (Exodus 10:3). And Canaan is father to them, for there is none below him. And it is not fitting to mingle holy with profane." "And the sense of \"the brother of Yefet the elder\": that Yefet was the better, and Scripture mentioned first the name of Shem, who is the younger, before Yefet, who is the elder, on account of his honor. And the proof is that Scripture says that Noah was five hundred years old when he began to beget, and there is no room here to object, for there are no fewer than five hundred years, and in the six-hundredth year the Flood came, and when Noah emerged from the Flood Shem was one hundred and one years old (Genesis 11:13 — one hundred and one years), and Scripture says (Genesis 11:10) two years after the Flood. And some interpreters say that \"the brother of Yefet the elder than him\" is Shem; therefore Scripture mentioned him first. And they say that when Shem was born, no more than a few days of Noah's five-hundredth year had elapsed, for a single day in a year counts as a year. And Noah was born in Iyyar and Shem in Sivan, and when the Flood came Noah was five hundred and ninety-nine years and some days, and so Shem did not have ninety-nine full years before the year of the Flood, and Shem would have been, when Arpachshad was born, one hundred years and some months. And the meaning of \"two years\" is that the second year entered by the reckoning from the beginning of the Flood. Also some say that five months after the day of the Flood, when the waters abated, Shem's wife conceived, and so Shem was one hundred years old. And according to this calculation it will be found in the Book of Kings in ten places."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :register {:flags {0 ["God"]}}, :notes {1 "Reading 'the better' (הטוב) for the text's שהיה יפת טוב, literally 'that Yefet was good'; some witnesses may read 'the elder' here but the logic requires contrast with Shem's precedence. Also, 'Yefet the elder than him' renders אחי יפת הגדול ממנו as some interpreters construe it. The final sentence about Kings is left as Ibn Ezra states it; the calculation's application remains cryptic."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:44:14.823925985Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}